readings.c1s1:
{{smaller block|The tother [man] was ȝalowere thene the ''ȝolke'' of a naye [an egg]. {{right|''Morte Arthure'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3284.}}}}
'''2.''' The vitellus, a part of the seed of plants, so named from its supposed analogy with the yolk of an egg.—'''3.''' The greasy sebaceous secretion or unctuous substance from the skin of the sheep, which renders the fleece soft and pliable; wool-oil.
{{smaller block|Is not the ''yoke'', or natural oiliness of the wool in the animal, more efficacious?
{{right|''Agric. Surv. of Galloway'', p. 283. (''Jamieson.'')}}}}

readings.c1s4:
¶

{{smaller block|<poem>O what hills are ''yon'', ''yon'' pleasant hills,
That the sun shines sweetly on?
“O ''yon'' are the hills of heaven,” he said.</poem>{{right|''The Dæmon Lover'' (Child’s Ballads, I. 203).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye see ''yon'' birkie ca’d a lord.</poem>{{right|''Burns'', For A’ That.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Behold her, single in the field,
''Yon'' solitary Highland Lass!</poem>{{right|''Wordsworth'', The Solitary Reaper.}}}}

'''yon''' (yon), ''adv.'' [An altered form of ''yond'', conformed to ''yon'', ''a.''] Same as ''yonder''.
{{smaller block|Him that ''yon'' soars on golden wing.{{right|''Milton'', Il Penseroso, l. 52.}}}}
'''Hither and yon.''' {{smaller|See ''hither''.}}

'''yond'''<sup>1</sup>† (yond), ''adv.'' and ''prep.'' [< ME. ''yond'', ''ȝond'', ''ȝund'', as prep. also ''ȝeond'', ''ȝend'', < AS. ''geond'' = LG. ''giend'' = Goth. ''jaind'', there; cf. ''yonder'', ''beyond'', and ''yon''.] '''I.''' ''adv.'' In or at that (more or less distant) place; yonder.
{{smaller block|<poem>And to the yonder hille I gan hire gyde,
Allas! and ther I took of hire my leeve,
And ''yonde'' I saugh hire to hire fader ryde.</poem>{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 612.}}}}
{{smaller block|Say what thou seest ''yond''.{{right|''Shak.'', Tempest, i. 2. 409.}}}}
'''II.''' ''prep.'' Through.
{{smaller block|''ȝond'' al the world.{{right|''Castell off Love'', l. 1448.}}}}

'''yond'''<sup>1</sup>† (yond), ''a.'' [< ME. ''yond'', ''ȝond'', ''ȝund'', ''ȝend''; a later form of ''yon'', made to agree with the adv. ''yond''.] Same as ''yon'' or ''yonder''.

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|Is ''yond'' your mistress?
{{right|''Middleton'' (''and others''), The Widow, iii. 3.}}}}

{{smaller block|And see ''yond'' fading Myrtle.
{{right|''Congreve'', Death of Queen Mary.}}}}

'''yond<sup>2</sup>†''' (yond), ''a.'' [Appar. one of Spenser’s inventions, a forced use of ''yond''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.''] Beside one’s self; mad; furious; insane. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Then like a Lyon . . . wexeth wood and ''yond''.
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., II. viii. 40.}}}}

'''yonder''' (yon′dėr), ''adv.'' [Also dial. ''yender''; < ME. ''yonder'', ''ȝonder'', ''ȝunder'', ''yender'', ''ȝender'' = MD. ''ghender'', ''ghinder'' = Goth. ''jaindre'', there; a compar. form of ''yon'', with suffix ''-der'' as in ''hither'', AS. ''hider'', ''under'', AS. ''under'', etc.] At or in that (more or less distant) place; at or in that place there.

{{smaller block|The felisshepe is yourez that ''yender'' ye see.
{{right|''Generydes'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 2869.}}}}

{{smaller block|Hold, ''yonder'' is some fellow skulking.
{{right|''Sheridan'', The Duenna, i. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|Chaucer uses the adverb frequently before the noun, and preceded by ''that'' or ''the'': a use indicating the transition to the adjective use:
<poem>In that ''yonder'' place
My lady first me took unto her grace.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, v. 580.}}}}

'''yonder''' (yon′dėr), ''a.'' [< ''yonder'', ''adv.'' Cf. ''yon''.] Being at a distance within view, or as conceived within view; that or those, referring to persons or things at a distance.

{{smaller block|<poem>Our pleasant labour to reform
Yon flowery arbours, ''yonder'' alleys green.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 626.}}}}

{{smaller block|Sweet Emma Moreland of ''yonder'' town}}
